The Walking Dead has been a whirlwind  success for AMC and its continued high  ratings are proof that the recent zombie  pop-culture craze is more than a fad but  the show has not been without its fair  share of issues both on screen and off  here's the behind-the-scenes history of  The Walking Dead  you might not know beam me up Robbie  when comic book writer Robert Kirkman  first came up with his concept for The  Walking Dead he didn't want his idea to  fester in some pile of generic zombie  pitches so he added a little something  extra to the mix aliens yes aliens  Kirkman revealed that when he first  approached publisher Image Comics about  his story ideas they thought his story  could use an extra hook so he threw in  an otherworldly element suggesting that  his zombies could have been sent to  Earth by aliens  Kirkman never intended to follow through  with that plot track but it got him the  greenlight ultimately Kirkman did circle  back on the alien theme in issue number  75 which features a bonus ending that  had some fun with that original concept  artist fights back Tony Moore whose  artwork appeared in many early issues of  Kirkman's comic series filed suit  against the author his childhood friend  after he felt duped into signing over  his financial rights to the franchise he  claimed he was owed a significant slice  of the brainy pie once the series hit it  big Kirkman disagreed and filed a  countersuit against Moore alleging he'd  breached their confidentiality agreement  with his filing the two amicably settled  their dispute in 2012 and more returned  to draw a variant cover for The Walking  Dead issue number 150 paying tribute to  his cover of issue number 1 which  originally debuted back in 2003 an intro  to remember a lot of things have changed  over the years on The Walking Dead but  the one thing that's remained the same  is that spooky intro music that lulls us  into some undead submission every  episode  [Music]  composure Bear McCreary designed the  iconic score and yes he intended for it  to be just that dreary  in a 2010 interview with mtv news he  explained that he wanted to capture a  sense of emptiness with the string notes  of his opening number as it was the  feeling he got from watching it he said  quote I wanted to create something that  would get stuck in your head  so that when you hear it you would think  of the walking dead mission accomplished  showrunner switcheroo Frank Darabont was  the Walking Dead's first showrunner and  his fingerprints are still all over the  place not only did he recruit the  original cast some of whom he'd worked  with before but he built the world  foundation upon which the show still  stands with Robert Kirkman so it was a  complete shock to him and everyone else  when the network decided to give him the  boot in season 2 dare Bob pursued legal  action against AMC accusing the network  of creating crisis level problems by  slashing the show's budget in spite of  rating success and accusing executives  of essentially inventing reasons to fire  him as of the making of this video  Darabont is seeking more than two  hundred and eighty million dollars in  damages Jeffrey deMunn who started in a  ton of Darabont's movies including  Shawshank Redemption The Green Mile and  the missed loyally followed Darabont  through the exit sign soon after his  untimely departure pre Daryl Dixon  Norman Reedus is crossbow wielding hero  Daryl Dixon has emerged as one of the  show's fan favorite characters but  Reedus originally auditioned for the  part of Merle which had already gone to  Michael Rooker showrunners liked to read  us so much though they created the part  of Daryl specifically for him talk about  a happy accident  the jail scene the prison setting might  have been fictional but some of the  Walking Dead's cast members have found  themselves behind bars in real life for  their extracurricular activities in 2012  actor Scott Wilson who played old man  Herschel in the show was arrested for  drunk driving then in 2015  Seth Gilliam who plays the good Reverend  Gabriel was also arrested for DUI and  possession of marijuana Sheriff Rick  must have been off duty those nights  thanks Carol Steven Yeun was born in  Seoul South Korea so his parents have  something of a language barrier when it  comes to fully understanding what's  happening on the show apparently in a  moment of confusion they told him to  make sure and thank co-star Melissa  McBride for that time Carol saved  everyone from the cannibals even after  Yun tried to explain that everything  happening on the show is fictional his  father insisted he thank her so he did  he Road Dogg Dooley the dog who played  Daryl and Beth's one-eyed canine  companion in season four was a real-life  hero he lost his right eye while  defending his owner from a carjacker  bravest cast member ever yup zombie  makeup levels with so many zombified  extras that play in any given episode  the makeup department has had to be  choosy as to which members of its crowd  get the full workup  so execs devise three levels of zombies  the highest is hero which gets the  full-body makeover like this guy make  sure we salon the back of his head and  the shoulders are really well and then  there's mid ground which warrants some  facial transformation and costuming  and when there's a horde of zombies on  screen many of the undead extras further  away from the camera just wear a zombie  mask and some ragged clothing no matter  the level though every extra has to go  to zombie school before filming to learn  how to move like a true biter mmm  what is that what about you I do it my  chief teaching of the zombies oh it's  pretty  thanks for watching subscribe to our  YouTube channel to see more videos like  the one you just saw and let us know in  the comments which Walking Dead facts  surprised you the most
 

